|~ The module of the noun `::qlang`, the language itself: its members are
   the concepts of the language, each a page read through the noun,
   `::qlang | doc :pipeline`, in the order they rest on each other:
   values, the pipeline, verbs, errors, code, the hypertext and the
   hosts. The first screen names them all. ~|

:values
  |~~ Values. Everything qlang handles is a value, and a value never
      changes: a step answers a new value and the old one stays as it
      was. Each value has a kind. A literal implies one, `42` a number,
      `"s"` a string, `:k` a keyword, `[a b]` a vector, `{:k v}` a map
      whose keys are keywords, `#[a b]` a set, `true`, `null`; a tag
      written over a value names another, `::Box{:k 1}`. A tag is the
      name of a kind: it tells how to read the value it stands on, tags
      stack and read from the outside in, and the tag taken as a value,
      `::Box`, is the kind itself, which a query asks for its page and
      its verbs. Code, prose and failures are values of kinds of their
      own, so everything the language says about itself is a value the
      pipeline reads. Every value prints as a literal that reads back as
      itself. Rests on nothing; the pipeline, the verbs and the
      hypertext rest on it.
      ::snippet~(::qlang | manifest) lists the kinds. ::link~(::tag | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :pipeline)

      Tags stack and read from the outside in:
      ~(::Box::Crate{:a 1} | type | eq ::Box)
      A step answers a new value and the old one stays as it was:
      ~([1 2] | :v / | append 3 | [v /] | eq [[1 2] [1 2 3]])
  ~~|
  #[::tag ::null ::boolean ::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set]

:pipeline
  |~~ The pipeline. A query is a subject and its steps; each step is a
      command on the value the step before it answered, `S | verb
      object`, and the answer is the last value. `a | b` runs `b` on
      `a`. `a * b`, written in the place of `|`, runs `b` on each element
      of a vector or each value of a map, several steps in parentheses,
      and answers the first failure. `/k`, `/0`, `/-1`, `/a/b` read a
      part, a miss being an error. `(…)` runs steps on the value in the
      pipe and answers their result, and each part of a literal is such
      a group, so a literal is the shape an answer takes,
      `{:id /id :n (/tags | count)}`. `:n v` names a value for the steps
      after it, in the scope where it stands. Rests on values; the verbs
      are its commands, and code holds its steps as a value.
      ::link~(::each | doc) ::link~(::proj | doc)
      ::link~(::group | doc) ::link~(::qlang | doc :verbs)

      `*` runs its step on each element:
      ~([{:id 1 :tags [1 2]}] * {:id /id :n (/tags | count)} | eq [{:id 1 :n 2}])
      A name holds the value in the pipe for the steps after it:
      ~(5 | :n / | [n (n | add 1)] | eq [5 6])
  ~~|
  #[::each ::proj ::group ::bind ::call ::construct]

:verbs
  |~~ Verbs. A verb lives on a kind, the one whose values it serves, and
      has an address, `::vec/sort`. A name in a query finds its verb
      through the kind of its subject: the tags from the outside in,
      then the shape beneath them, then the verbs every value answers,
      on `::qlang/any`; so what can be done with a value is asked of its
      kind, and one word serves many kinds. The verbs of a kind and those
      of every value are the whole of them: a verb in neither list does
      not exist. A verb declares its slots and the kinds it takes, which
      `spec` answers before a call. A reader declares verbs and kinds of
      its own in the form the catalog is written in: a name over `::verb`
      and a quote whose head declares its slots is a verb, and a tag
      named over a map whose `:impl` is a quote is a kind, whose
      constructor checks or builds each value put under it and answers
      an error for one it refuses. Rests on
      values and the pipeline; errors name the verb that refused.
      ::snippet~(::vec | spec | /verbs) lists the verbs of a kind and
      ::snippet~(::qlang/any | spec | /verbs) those of every value.
      ::link~(::verb | doc) ::link~(::qlang | doc :errors)

      A verb declared with a slot takes its argument after its name:
      ~(:inc ::verb~(:by ::number | add by) | 5 | inc 2 | eq 7)
      A verb answers its signature by `spec`:
      ~(::vec/sort | spec | type | eq ::spec)
      A kind declared with a constructor checks its values:
      ~(::Small {:impl ~(if (lt 10) ~(/) ~(!{:reason :tooBig}))} | [(::Small(3) | type) (::Small(12) !| /reason)] | eq [::Small :tooBig])
  ~~|
  #[::verb ::spec ::qlang/any]

:errors
  |~~ Errors. A failure does not throw: a step that fails answers an
      error, a value that carries the facts of the failure, the path it
      took and the tag of the site that refused, which names the page of
      its procedure. `|` passes an error by and `*` stops on one;
      `a !| b` runs `b` on an error alone, opened to its facts, so a query
      reads a failure, recovers from it or raises it again where it is
      read. `!| type` names the site, and `!| explain` shows its page
      above the error. A reader learning a tool fails often, and the
      failure is where what it needs is cheapest to give. Rests on
      values, the pipeline and verbs; the hypertext holds the pages the
      sites name.
      ::link~(::error | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::explanation | doc)

      A failed step answers an error the tag of its site names:
      ~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::AddLeftNotNumberError)
      `!|` recovers each failure where it is read:
      ~([1 "x"] * (mul 2 !| 0) | eq [2 0])
  ~~|
  #[::error ::fail ::explanation]

:code
  |~~ Code. A quote, `~(…)`, holds steps unrun as a value: a verb takes
      one where it needs code, `filter`, `sort`, `if`, `apply` runs one on
      a subject, and a quote is taken apart and built like any vector of
      steps. Because code is a value, a page holds laws that run and
      links that open, and the trail of an error holds the steps it
      skipped, ready to replay. Rests on values and the pipeline; the
      hypertext is written in it.
      ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::qlang | doc :hypertext)

      A quote held as a value runs by `apply`:
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | :q / | 5 | apply q | eq 12)
      A quote is the vector of its steps:
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 2)
  ~~|
  #[::quote]

:hypertext
  |~~ The hypertext. The names are a space of addresses, `::` beginning
      an absolute one, `::vec/sort`, and a verb passes from an address
      into one of its projections, as a path reaches a file: `doc` into
      its page, `spec` into its declaration, `source` into the code that
      declared it, and `runLaws` runs the laws of its page. An address holds no one
      entity but a set of values, and each passage reads one of them; the
      address, the verb and what it reaches are all values, so the
      pipeline goes on from a projection as from any value, and a tag
      you declare with verbs on it adds projections of its own.
      A keyword after `doc` is an anchor within the page of its subject,
      `::qlang | doc :pipeline`, a vector of anchors a path, a set of
      anchors several pages at once. A page is a doc, prose that holds
      code: a quote with no tag is a law, which `runLaws` holds to
      answer true, a quote under `::link` is a link to another page, and
      a quote under `::snippet` a query a reader runs to see what it
      shows; `open` runs either. So a page leads on to the pages that go deeper,
      and the pipeline moves through them as through any data:
      `manifest` lists the kinds, `spec | /verbs` the verbs of a kind,
      `links * open` opens every page a page names in one query, and a
      filter over pages by `content | contains` searches them. Nothing
      is read that was not asked for, and a query names the shape of
      what it reads. Rests on every concept before it.
      ::link~(::doc | doc) ::link~(::link | doc) ::link~(::snippet | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :hosts)

      The links of a page open in one query:
      ~(::qlang | doc | links * open | count | gt 0)
      One address passes into its page, its declaration and its source:
      ~(::vec/take | [(doc | type) (spec | type) (source | type)] | eq [::doc ::spec ::quote])
      A filter over pages searches them:
      ~(::vec | spec | /verbs | filter ~(doc | content | contains "after the first") | eq #[::vec/drop])
  ~~|
  #[::doc ::link ::snippet]

:hosts
  |~~ Hosts. The language runs inside a host, a tool that embeds it and
      brings verbs of its own; the command line is one. A value is whole
      in the language, and the host prints an answer whole unless a
      reader asks for a budget, by `elide` in the query or by the host's
      flag, the command line's `--budget=N`: what a budget left out is
      an `::elision` marker, whose `:read` continues the query to the
      part. The command line reads JSON or text from stdin and writes
      its answer back in the same form. Rests on values and the hypertext.
      ::link~(::elision | doc) ::link~(::any/elide | doc)

      A value whose print fits its budget is answered as it is:
      ~([1 2] | elide 100 | eq [1 2])
  ~~|
  #[::elision]
